      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}m&=\\frac{-6-(-3)}{1-0}\\\\m&=-3\\end{align*}$$\nSince $f(0)=-3$, the $y$-intercept is $-3$, giving\n$$f(x)=-3x-3.$$\nMultiplying the domain inequality by $-3$ reverses its direction:\n$$\\begin{align*}x&\\le2\\\\-3x&\\ge-6\\\\-3x-3&\\ge-9\\\\f(x)&\\ge-9\\end{align*}$$\nThe negative gradient means the function decreases to its included minimum $f(2)=-9$. As $x$ decreases without bound, $f(x)$ increases without bound. Every $y\\ge-9$ is attained at $x=-\\frac{y+3}{3}\\le2$.\nTherefore the range is $[-9,\\infty)$.",
